[[:db/retract 17592186146834 :answer-field/choices 17592186146835] [:db/retract 17592186146834 :answer-field/choices 17592186146836] [:db/retract 17592186146834 :answer-field/choices 17592186146837] [:db/retract 17592186146834 :answer-field/choices 17592186146838] [:db/retract 17592186146834 :answer-field/choices 17592186146839] {:db/id 17592186147444 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-76c1e10b-ff92-55c3-9f48-cde089c3ec98"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147372 :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?\n\nI. $y''+4y' - y = 0$\n\nII. $y''+ 4 = 0$\n\nIII. $y''+y'+4y = 0$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d285631-ec24-50a4-ab3c-c0601632cbad"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147373 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n\nII. $y'' +2y'+ y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nIII. $y'' +4y'+3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147446 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-db14497d-6b9b-508e-a43a-c6705f9b9e88"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147447 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6c4b5efd-8648-5dd6-b5f3-7b3ca42bb25b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-76c1e10b-ff92-55c3-9f48-cde089c3ec98" :question/id #uuid "76c1e10b-ff92-55c3-9f48-cde089c3ec98" :question/math-academy-id "q-64587" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bc9a2aa2-4314-5e20-994f-9b028b117d14"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc9a2aa2-4314-5e20-994f-9b028b117d14" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9a2aa2-4314-5e20-994f-9b028b117d14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fa80ea3b-48bf-5ceb-ae4d-04796c4fd4de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4937738-4f40-5a50-b1b9-b581e2efc232" "answer-ef917fb3-8de5-5591-8539-94e826aa4bd7" "answer-f363187b-6c07-55e7-8bd6-d9fde1f982ca" "answer-fa80ea3b-48bf-5ceb-ae4d-04796c4fd4de" "answer-23192ffb-c5ff-5772-abd5-beb8dbc77391"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4937738-4f40-5a50-b1b9-b581e2efc232" :answer/id #uuid "f4937738-4f40-5a50-b1b9-b581e2efc232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+16y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef917fb3-8de5-5591-8539-94e826aa4bd7" :answer/id #uuid "ef917fb3-8de5-5591-8539-94e826aa4bd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+25y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f363187b-6c07-55e7-8bd6-d9fde1f982ca" :answer/id #uuid "f363187b-6c07-55e7-8bd6-d9fde1f982ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-9y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa80ea3b-48bf-5ceb-ae4d-04796c4fd4de" :answer/id #uuid "fa80ea3b-48bf-5ceb-ae4d-04796c4fd4de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23192ffb-c5ff-5772-abd5-beb8dbc77391" :answer/id #uuid "23192ffb-c5ff-5772-abd5-beb8dbc77391" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y={e}^{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186357723 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d285631-ec24-50a4-ab3c-c0601632cbad" :question/id #uuid "9d285631-ec24-50a4-ab3c-c0601632cbad" :question/math-academy-id "q-94314" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+36y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(4) & =48>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+36y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={12}^{2}-4(1)(36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-578e80cb-b054-52ce-97be-509dd668d752"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-578e80cb-b054-52ce-97be-509dd668d752" :answer-field/id #uuid "578e80cb-b054-52ce-97be-509dd668d752" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-902fe176-8794-5e7f-a141-85bc2b1d225d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d225cf09-2533-5d78-b568-46029a1627a0" "answer-635d6934-78cc-51ab-a251-c7a8c8ed0bb2" "answer-2a699d4d-481b-5164-8a94-fe6421c0ee70" "answer-902fe176-8794-5e7f-a141-85bc2b1d225d" "answer-31ffbbd1-678b-5808-8fca-cdd7644b7978"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d225cf09-2533-5d78-b568-46029a1627a0" :answer/id #uuid "d225cf09-2533-5d78-b568-46029a1627a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-635d6934-78cc-51ab-a251-c7a8c8ed0bb2" :answer/id #uuid "635d6934-78cc-51ab-a251-c7a8c8ed0bb2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a699d4d-481b-5164-8a94-fe6421c0ee70" :answer/id #uuid "2a699d4d-481b-5164-8a94-fe6421c0ee70" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-902fe176-8794-5e7f-a141-85bc2b1d225d" :answer/id #uuid "902fe176-8794-5e7f-a141-85bc2b1d225d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31ffbbd1-678b-5808-8fca-cdd7644b7978" :answer/id #uuid "31ffbbd1-678b-5808-8fca-cdd7644b7978" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148179 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186357725 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186335726 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-db14497d-6b9b-508e-a43a-c6705f9b9e88" :question/id #uuid "db14497d-6b9b-508e-a43a-c6705f9b9e88" :question/math-academy-id "q-94320" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=6\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-6{y}^{′}-18y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+6y=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4bf5f75c-ce4c-503a-9ca3-208934e1466a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4bf5f75c-ce4c-503a-9ca3-208934e1466a" :answer-field/id #uuid "4bf5f75c-ce4c-503a-9ca3-208934e1466a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2f217b91-26db-54d6-8126-a7bfbc2461c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8aaa50e-b1c0-5da1-be48-5bf477ec6ca1" "answer-2f217b91-26db-54d6-8126-a7bfbc2461c6" "answer-7e16aa15-0013-5d80-9600-471158f1dfa4" "answer-f64c5d26-0b90-5645-b4d8-a2cc1ce6a4ce" "answer-b6f560a0-d4b7-5674-91a8-c59f28f39b92"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8aaa50e-b1c0-5da1-be48-5bf477ec6ca1" :answer/id #uuid "d8aaa50e-b1c0-5da1-be48-5bf477ec6ca1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+3\\sqrt{2}\\,y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f217b91-26db-54d6-8126-a7bfbc2461c6" :answer/id #uuid "2f217b91-26db-54d6-8126-a7bfbc2461c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+6y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e16aa15-0013-5d80-9600-471158f1dfa4" :answer/id #uuid "7e16aa15-0013-5d80-9600-471158f1dfa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+18y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f64c5d26-0b90-5645-b4d8-a2cc1ce6a4ce" :answer/id #uuid "f64c5d26-0b90-5645-b4d8-a2cc1ce6a4ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6f560a0-d4b7-5674-91a8-c59f28f39b92" :answer/id #uuid "b6f560a0-d4b7-5674-91a8-c59f28f39b92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148180 :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+13y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+13 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=2\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{2}=\\pi \\,\\text{s}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186146834 :answer-field/correct "answer-d2951689-73d1-537c-aae4-36d5122518f6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-908ef03e-2769-52ca-bdea-efd9e7c58c33" "answer-e631dceb-185d-59ac-ba82-82d589019dfe" "answer-e4e1684d-8e00-5782-9e5e-23e3c0e032c6" "answer-dca0d751-a78d-50e2-8aa2-db827b1d277c" "answer-d2951689-73d1-537c-aae4-36d5122518f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-908ef03e-2769-52ca-bdea-efd9e7c58c33" :answer/id #uuid "908ef03e-2769-52ca-bdea-efd9e7c58c33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e631dceb-185d-59ac-ba82-82d589019dfe" :answer/id #uuid "e631dceb-185d-59ac-ba82-82d589019dfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4e1684d-8e00-5782-9e5e-23e3c0e032c6" :answer/id #uuid "e4e1684d-8e00-5782-9e5e-23e3c0e032c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dca0d751-a78d-50e2-8aa2-db827b1d277c" :answer/id #uuid "dca0d751-a78d-50e2-8aa2-db827b1d277c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2951689-73d1-537c-aae4-36d5122518f6" :answer/id #uuid "d2951689-73d1-537c-aae4-36d5122518f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6c4b5efd-8648-5dd6-b5f3-7b3ca42bb25b" :question/id #uuid "6c4b5efd-8648-5dd6-b5f3-7b3ca42bb25b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94360" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=4\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8174c21b-eafc-56be-ad59-cefe6b8b533d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8174c21b-eafc-56be-ad59-cefe6b8b533d" :answer-field/id #uuid "8174c21b-eafc-56be-ad59-cefe6b8b533d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e3b28ba7-4b44-5c62-bebc-3289f103e700" :answer-field/choices ["answer-12588678-dc2f-56ca-b7ad-d201ff8cd771" "answer-8db5b483-0165-56b2-a792-56ccfc28ac64" "answer-e3b28ba7-4b44-5c62-bebc-3289f103e700" "answer-01d92886-a0b6-558c-8d1d-01e1a39fc792" "answer-61f58f09-3464-5d14-a7fc-6b2c92612ed2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12588678-dc2f-56ca-b7ad-d201ff8cd771" :answer/id #uuid "12588678-dc2f-56ca-b7ad-d201ff8cd771" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8db5b483-0165-56b2-a792-56ccfc28ac64" :answer/id #uuid "8db5b483-0165-56b2-a792-56ccfc28ac64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3b28ba7-4b44-5c62-bebc-3289f103e700" :answer/id #uuid "e3b28ba7-4b44-5c62-bebc-3289f103e700" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01d92886-a0b6-558c-8d1d-01e1a39fc792" :answer/id #uuid "01d92886-a0b6-558c-8d1d-01e1a39fc792" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61f58f09-3464-5d14-a7fc-6b2c92612ed2" :answer/id #uuid "61f58f09-3464-5d14-a7fc-6b2c92612ed2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147704 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
